[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
**Mapbox GL JS (v5-style)**  

```js
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';
```

**MapLibre GL JS v6 equivalent**

```js
// ✅ Namespace import (most common)
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// or, if you only need the Map class (tree-shakable)
import { Map, NavigationControl } from 'maplibre-gl';
```

### Why the change?

* **v6 removed the default export** – `import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl'` (or `import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'`) no longer works.
* Use a **named or namespace import** instead, as shown above.
* (Optional but recommended) **Pin the version** you’re loading, e.g.:

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl@^6.0.0';
```

Now you can create a map just like with Mapbox GL JS:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',          // id of the HTML element
  style: 'https://example.com/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

**Important note – v6 of MapLibre GL JS no longer ships a CommonJS entry point.**  
Calling

```js
const maplibregl = require('maplibre-gl');
```

in a plain Node CommonJS file now throws `ERR_PACKAGE_PATH_NOT_EXPORTED`.  
You have three practical ways to get the version from a Node script:

| What you want | How to do it (still a CommonJS file) |
|---------------|---------------------------------------|
| **Stay on v5** (the last version that supported `require`) | `npm i maplibre-gl@5` and keep the `require` call. |
| **Upgrade to v6 but keep a CommonJS file** | Use a *dynamic* `import()` (which returns a Promise) inside the CommonJS module. |
| **Switch the whole file to ESM** | Rename the file to `.mjs` or add `"type":"module"` in `package.json` and use a static `import`. |

Below are two ready-to-run examples that work with **MapLibre GL JS v6** while the file itself remains a CommonJS module.

---

## 1️⃣ CommonJS file that uses a dynamic `import()` (Node ≥ 14)

```js
// file: print-maplibre-version.cjs   (or any .js file run with `node`)

(async () => {
  try {
    // Dynamically import the ESM build – this works from a CJS context
    const maplibregl = await import('maplibre-gl');

    // The library still exposes a static `version` property
    console.log('MapLibre GL JS version:', maplibregl.version);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load maplibre-gl:', err);
    process.exit(1);
  }
})();
```

Run it with:

```bash
node print-maplibre-version.cjs
```

**Why it works**

* `import('maplibre-gl')` returns a Promise that resolves to the module’s namespace object (`* as maplibregl`).  
* The namespace contains the same public API as the old default export, including `maplibregl.version`.

---

## 2️⃣ If you only need the version number, read the package’s `package.json`

Even when the module itself is ESM-only, the JSON file on disk is still readable with the built-in `fs` module:

```js
// file: print-maplibre-version.cjs
const { readFileSync } = require('fs');
const { join } = require('path');

try {
  // Resolve the location of the installed package
  const pkgPath = require.resolve('maplibre-gl/package.json');
  const pkgJson = JSON.parse(readFileSync(pkgPath, 'utf8'));

  console.log('MapLibre GL JS version (from package.json):', pkgJson.version);
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Could not read maplibre-gl package.json:', err);
  process.exit(1);
}
```

Run the same way:

```bash
node print-maplibre-version.cjs
```

This approach never touches the library’s JavaScript entry point, so it works regardless of the module format.

---

## 3️⃣ (Optional) Switch the whole script to ESM

If you prefer the classic static import syntax, rename the file to `.mjs` (or set `"type":"module"` in your `package.json`) and write:

```js
// file: print-maplibre-version.mjs
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

console.log('MapLibre GL JS version:', maplibregl.version);
```

```bash
node print-maplibre-version.mjs
```

---

### TL;DR

* **`require('maplibre-gl')` is no longer possible in v6.**  
* Use a **dynamic `import()`** inside your CommonJS script, or read the version from `package.json`, or downgrade to v5 if you must stay with `require`.  

Pick the pattern that best fits the rest of your build pipeline. Happy mapping!

### The v6 way to supply a missing icon at runtime  

In MapLibre GL JS v5 you could do this:

```js
map.on('styleimagemissing', e => {
  map.addImage(e.id, myGeneratedImage);
});
```

In **v6** the `styleimagemissing` event is **notify-only** – calling `addImage()` from the handler no longer resolves the pending request, so the icon never appears.  
Instead you register a **missing-style-image resolver** with `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver()`.  
The resolver is called whenever the style asks for an image that isn’t already in the sprite; you add the image yourself and the map will automatically use it.

---

## Step-by-step example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Dynamic missing-icon example (v6)</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  // 1️⃣ Import the ESM build (CDN example – pin a major version!)
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  // 2️⃣ Create the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        osm: { type: 'raster', tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'], tileSize: 256 }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'raster',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'osm'
        },
        // A symbol layer that references an icon that does NOT exist in the sprite
        {
          id: 'dynamic-points',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: 'osm',
          layout: {
            'icon-image': 'my-dynamic-icon',   // <-- not in the sprite
            'icon-size': 1.5
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // 3️⃣ Register the resolver **once**, after the map instance exists
  map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (missingId) => {
    // `missingId` is the string the style asked for (e.g. "my-dynamic-icon")
    console.log('Missing image requested:', missingId);

    // ----> Your custom logic here <----
    // You can:
    //   * fetch a PNG/SVG from a server,
    //   * generate a canvas or ImageBitmap on the fly,
    //   * use any async work you need.
    // Below is a tiny example that draws a red circle on a canvas.

    const size = 64;                     // pixel size of the generated icon
    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
    canvas.width = canvas.height = size;
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

    // draw a red circle
    ctx.fillStyle = 'red';
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(size / 2, size / 2, size / 2 - 4, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();

    // Convert the canvas to an ImageBitmap (preferred for performance)
    const imageBitmap = await createImageBitmap(canvas);

    // 4️⃣ Add the image to the map. The map will automatically resolve the pending request.
    map.addImage(missingId, imageBitmap, { sdf: false });

    // The resolver does **not** need to return anything.
    // If you want to abort the request, simply do nothing (or throw).
  });

  // Optional: listen to the old event just for debugging (it still fires)
  map.on('styleimagemissing', e => {
    console.log('styleimagemissing event (notify-only):', e.id);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Import** | v6 only ships an ESM build (`dist/maplibre-gl.mjs`). Use `type="module"` and pin a version (`@^6.0.0`). |
| **Create map** | Any normal `new maplibregl.Map(...)` works. The style contains a `symbol` layer that references `icon-image: 'my-dynamic-icon'`, which is *not* in the sprite. |
| **`setMissingStyleImageResolver`** | Registers a **single** async callback that will be invoked for every missing image request. Inside you generate or fetch the image and call `map.addImage(id, image)`. Once `addImage` resolves, the map automatically uses the newly-added icon. |
| **`styleimagemissing` event** | Still emitted, but it’s only a notification. You can keep a listener for logging, but you must **not** rely on it to resolve the image. |
| **Image generation** | The example draws a red circle on a canvas, converts it to an `ImageBitmap`, and adds it. Replace this with `await fetch(...).then(r=>r.blob()).then(createImageBitmap)` or any other async source you need. |

---

## Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Issue | Why it happens in v6 | Fix |
|-------|----------------------|-----|
| `map.addImage()` inside a `styleimagemissing` handler does nothing | The event is now *notify-only*; the pending request is not linked to the handler. | Use `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver()` instead. |
| The resolver never runs | You called `setMissingStyleImageResolver` **after** the map has already emitted the missing-image request (e.g., before the map is fully constructed). | Register the resolver **immediately after** creating the `Map` instance, before any layers that reference unknown icons are added, or simply call it right after `new Map(...)` as shown. |
| The generated image is blurry or missing | You added a plain `<canvas>` element instead of an `ImageBitmap`/`HTMLImageElement`, or you omitted the `sdf` flag when needed. | Use `await createImageBitmap(canvas)` (or `new Image()` with `src` set) and pass the correct options (`{sdf: true}` for signed-distance-field icons). |
| TypeScript complains “`setMissingStyleImageResolver` does not exist” | Using an older `@types/maplibre-gl` or a v5 version of the library. | Make sure you have **MapLibre GL JS v6** installed (`npm i maplibre-gl@^6`) and that your `tsconfig` resolves the package’s own types (no external `@types` needed). |
| The map never renders when bundling (Vite/webpack) | The worker URL isn’t set; the map stalls before the resolver can run. | For bundler builds you still need `setWorkerUrl()` (see the migration guide). The CDN example above does **not** need it. |

---

## TL;DR code snippet (minimal)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({ container: 'map', style: myStyle });

map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async id => {
  // fetch or generate the image
  const img = await fetch(`/icons/${id}.png`).then(r => r.blob())
               .then(b => createImageBitmap(b));
  map.addImage(id, img);
});
```

Whenever the style asks for an icon that isn’t in the sprite, the resolver runs, adds the image, and the map instantly displays it. This is the **official v6 replacement** for the old `styleimagemissing`-plus-`addImage` pattern.

Below is a minimal, **v6-only** example that shows how to read every piece of the camera state you asked for:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – camera state + projection matrix</title>
  <link
    href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
    rel="stylesheet"
  />
  <style>
    #map { position: absolute; inset: 0; }
    #info { position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; margin: 8px;
            background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9); padding: 8px;
            font-family: monospace; font-size: 12px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<div id="info"></div>

<script type="module">
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Import the v6 ESM build (no default export)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  import {
    Map,
    // (optional) you can import only what you need:
    // NavigationControl,
  } from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Create the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 45,
    bearing: 30,
    // For a CDN-imported map you **do not** need setWorkerUrl()
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Helper to dump the public camera values
  // -------------------------------------------------
  function dumpCameraState() {
    const center = map.getCenter();          // → LngLat object
    const zoom   = map.getZoom();            // number
    const bearing = map.getBearing();        // degrees
    const pitch   = map.getPitch();          // degrees

    return {
      center: [center.lng, center.lat],
      zoom,
      bearing,
      pitch,
    };
  }

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Custom layer that captures the projection matrix
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const matrixLayer = {
    id: 'matrix-capture',
    type: 'custom',
    // The layer is always rendered after the map has updated its camera,
    // so the matrix we receive is the *current* one.
    render(gl, matrix) {
      // `matrix` is a Float32Array[16] column-major 4×4 projection matrix.
      // Store it where you need it (here we just keep a global variable).
      window.currentProjectionMatrix = matrix.slice(); // copy for safety

      // (optional) draw something with the matrix – here we do nothing.
      // The layer must call `gl.flush()` if you issue GL commands.
    }
  };

  map.addLayer(matrixLayer);

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  Show the state in the UI whenever the camera changes
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const infoEl = document.getElementById('info');

  function updateInfo() {
    const cam = dumpCameraState();
    const mat = window.currentProjectionMatrix
      ? Array.from(window.currentProjectionMatrix).map(v => v.toFixed(3))
      : ['(not rendered yet)'];

    infoEl.innerHTML = `
      <strong>Camera</strong><br>
      center:  ${cam.center.map(v => v.toFixed(5)).join(', ')}<br>
      zoom:    ${cam.zoom.toFixed(3)}<br>
      bearing: ${cam.bearing.toFixed(2)}°<br>
      pitch:   ${cam.pitch.toFixed(2)}°<br><br>
      <strong>Projection matrix (first row)</strong><br>
      ${mat.slice(0, 4).join(', ')}
    `;
  }

  // Update on any camera-changing event
  map.on('move', updateInfo);
  map.on('zoom', updateInfo);
  map.on('rotate', updateInfo);
  map.on('pitch', updateInfo);
  // The matrix is only available after the first render,
  // so also update after the map becomes idle.
  map.on('idle', updateInfo);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Piece | How you obtain it (v6) |
|------|------------------------|
| **center** | `map.getCenter()` → `LngLat` (use `.lng` / `.lat` or spread to an array) |
| **zoom**   | `map.getZoom()` |
| **bearing**| `map.getBearing()` (degrees) |
| **pitch**  | `map.getPitch()` (degrees) |
| **projection matrix** | Inside a **custom layer** `render(gl, matrix)` the second argument **is** the 4 × 4 projection matrix (a `Float32Array` of length 16). Store or use it however you need. |

#### Why we use a custom layer for the matrix

* In v5 the internal `map.transform` object exposed `projMatrix`, but that property was removed in v6.  
* The public API now only hands the matrix to you through the custom-layer rendering callback. Outside of a custom layer there is no official way to read the raw matrix; you must capture it as shown above.

If you only need the *public* camera values (center, zoom, bearing, pitch) you can rely on the getter methods alone. When you also need the exact projection matrix for your own math, the custom-layer approach is the supported, future-proof method.

```ts
// map.ts – a type-safe “data” handler for MapLibre GL JS v6

import type {
  Map,
  MapSourceDataEvent,
  MapStyleDataEvent,
} from 'maplibre-gl';

// `map` is your already-created MapLibre map instance
declare const map: Map;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper type guard – tells TypeScript that the event is a source-data
// event (it has the `sourceDataType` property).
function isSourceDataEvent(
  e: MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent,
): e is MapSourceDataEvent {
  return 'sourceDataType' in e;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// The actual handler
function onMapData(e: MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent) {
  if (isSourceDataEvent(e)) {
    // ✅ This branch is a MapSourceDataEvent – we can safely read
    //    `sourceDataType`, `sourceId`, `tile`, etc.
    switch (e.sourceDataType) {
      case 'metadata':
        console.log('Style metadata loaded', e);
        break;

      case 'source':
        console.log(`Source "${e.sourceId}" finished loading`, e);
        break;

      case 'tile':
        console.log(
          `Tile ${e.tile?.z}/${e.tile?.x}/${e.tile?.y} of source "${e.sourceId}" loaded`,
          e,
        );
        break;

      default:
        // If MapLibre adds new sourceDataType values in the future,
        // this fallback keeps the code type-safe.
        console.warn('Unhandled sourceDataType:', (e as any).sourceDataType);
    }
  } else {
    // ✅ This branch is a MapStyleDataEvent – it has no `sourceDataType`
    //    but does expose `styleId` and `dataType`.
    console.log('Style data event:', e);
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Register the handler
map.on('data', onMapData);
```

### What’s happening?

* **Import the correct event types** – `MapDataEvent` was removed in v6; the
  `data`, `dataloading`, and `dataabort` events now emit a union of
  `MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent`.
* **Narrow with a type guard** – checking for the presence of
  `sourceDataType` (or any other property unique to `MapSourceDataEvent`)
  tells TypeScript which side of the union you’re dealing with.
* **Switch on `sourceDataType`** – now you can safely handle the different
  source-data phases (`metadata`, `source`, `tile`, …) with full type safety.
* The `else` branch handles style-related data events, which lack
  `sourceDataType`.

This pattern gives you compile-time guarantees that you only access properties
that actually exist on the concrete event type.

Below is a complete, **v6-only** example that shows how to:

1. Load a **PMTiles** file (a single-file container for vector tiles)  
2. Register the `pmtiles://` protocol that MapLibre understands  
3. Add the vector source to the map  
4. Draw a simple **line** layer from one of the source’s layers  

You can run the same code in a plain HTML page (CDN) or inside a bundler-based project (Vite, webpack, etc.). The only difference is the way the MapLibre worker is set up – the CDN version needs no extra call, a bundler does (see the short note at the end).

---

## 1️⃣  CDN (no build step) – the simplest way to try it out

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  // ---- 1️⃣ import the two libraries (ESM build) -------------------------
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
  import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3/+esm';

  // ---- 2️⃣ Register the pmtiles:// protocol ----------------------------
  // The PMTiles constructor takes the URL of the .pmtiles file.
  const pmtiles = new PMTiles('https://YOUR_DOMAIN/path/to/your-data.pmtiles');

  // maplibregl.addProtocol registers a custom fetch handler.
  // The handler is called for any URL that starts with "pmtiles://".
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtiles.protocol);

  // ---- 3️⃣ Create the map ------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style you like
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // Wait until the style is loaded before adding our own source/layer
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // ---- 4️⃣ Add the vector source that points to the PMTiles file -------
    map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
      type: 'vector',
      // The URL uses the custom protocol we just registered.
      // The part after "pmtiles://" is the exact same string you passed to
      // the PMTiles constructor above.
      url: 'pmtiles://https://YOUR_DOMAIN/path/to/your-data.pmtiles'
    });

    // ---- 5️⃣ Add a line layer that draws from a source-layer inside the file
    // Replace "my_line_layer" with the actual layer name that exists in the
    // vector tile source (you can inspect it with `pmtiles inspect` or a
    // viewer like https://github.com/protomaps/PMTilesViewer).
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
      'source-layer': 'my_line_layer',   // <-- name of the layer inside the tileset
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#ff6600',
        'line-width': 2,
        'line-opacity': 0.8
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What’s happening**

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Import** | Pulls the ESM-only MapLibre build (`maplibre-gl.mjs`) and the PMTiles helper (`PMTiles`). |
| **Protocol registration** | `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtiles.protocol)` tells MapLibre how to fetch `pmtiles://…` URLs. |
| **Map creation** | Any valid style works; the demo uses the public MapLibre demo style. |
| **Source** | `type: 'vector'` + `url: 'pmtiles://…'` points the source at the PMTiles file. |
| **Layer** | A normal `line` layer that references the source-layer inside the tileset. |

> **Tip:** If you’re not sure what source-layer names exist in the PMTiles file, open the file in the **PMTiles Viewer** (https://github.com/protomaps/PMTilesViewer) or run `pmtiles inspect your-data.pmtiles` on the command line.

---

## 2️⃣  Bundler (Vite, webpack, esbuild, …) – typical dev setup

If you’re using a bundler you’ll install the packages locally:

```bash
npm i maplibre-gl pmtiles
```

Then in your source file (e.g. `src/main.ts` or `src/main.js`):

```ts
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Imports
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
import { Map, setWorkerUrl, addProtocol } from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
import { PMTiles } from 'pmtiles';

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Worker URL – required for bundlers (Vite example)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣ Register the pmtiles protocol
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
const pmtiles = new PMTiles('https://YOUR_DOMAIN/path/to/your-data.pmtiles');
addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtiles.protocol);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣ Initialise the map
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',               // <div id="map"></div> in your HTML
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

map.on('style.load', () => {
  // -----------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣ Add the vector source
  // -----------------------------------------------------------
  map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
    type: 'vector',
    url: 'pmtiles://https://YOUR_DOMAIN/path/to/your-data.pmtiles'
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------
  // 6️⃣ Add the line layer
  // -----------------------------------------------------------
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-lines',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
    'source-layer': 'my_line_layer', // <-- adjust to your data
    paint: {
      'line-color': '#0066ff',
      'line-width': 3,
      'line-opacity': 0.9
    }
  });
});
```

**Key differences from the CDN version**

| Item | CDN | Bundler |
|------|-----|---------|
| **Import style** | `import * as maplibregl from '…/maplibre-gl.mjs'` | `import { Map, setWorkerUrl, addProtocol } from 'maplibre-gl'` |
| **Worker URL** | Auto-detected, no extra code | Must call `setWorkerUrl()` once (see snippet) |
| **CSS** | Linked via `<link>` tag | Imported directly (`import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css'`) |
| **Protocol registration** | `maplibregl.addProtocol` | `addProtocol` (re-exported from the same package) |

---

## 3️⃣  Quick checklist – “Did I forget something?”

| ✅ | Checklist item |
|---|-----------------|
| **PMTiles file is publicly reachable** (CORS headers allow `GET` from your page). |
| **`source-layer` name matches exactly** the layer name inside the vector tileset. |
| **`pmtiles://` URL uses the same full URL you passed to `new PMTiles(...)`.** |
| **If you’re using a bundler** – `setWorkerUrl()` has been called **before** creating the `Map`. |
| **MapLibre CSS is loaded** (otherwise the map container will be invisible). |
| **Map container (`<div id="map">`) has a size** (width/height > 0). |

---

## 4️⃣  Optional: Styling the line further

You can make the line look more “road-like” by adding a **line-gradient**, **line-dasharray**, or **line-blur**:

```js
paint: {
  'line-color': '#ff6600',
  'line-width': [
    'interpolate',
    ['linear'],
    ['zoom'],
    5, 1,
    12, 4,
    15, 8
  ],
  'line-opacity': 0.9,
  'line-dasharray': [2, 4],   // dashed line
  'line-blur': 0.5
}
```

All of these are standard MapLibre style properties – they work exactly the same whether the source is a PMTiles file or any other vector source.

---

### TL;DR

* **Register the `pmtiles://` protocol** with `addProtocol` (or `maplibregl.addProtocol`).  
* **Add a vector source** that points to the PMTiles file via `url: 'pmtiles://…'`.  
* **Create a line layer** that references the source and the appropriate `source-layer`.  

That’s all you need to get a line layer rendered from a PMTiles vector-tile file in MapLibre GL JS v6. Happy mapping!
